use /lib
use /ui
from /ui/components/scrollbar import ScrollbarScope

export ui-component FsBrowserScope:
    concept: {FsBrowserConcept}

export concept FsBrowserConcept:
    pitch:
        A browser of the file system drawn by the application itself rather
        than the platform's native dialog, so it looks and behaves the same
        everywhere and sits inside whatever panel needs it. What it lists, and
        what it chooses, is up to each kind of browser built on it.

    shape: {FsBrowserShape}

export shape FsBrowserShape:
    description:
        A title saying what is being chosen and for what, a bar for getting
        around, the entries in the current folder, and buttons to choose or
        cancel along the bottom. It starts in whatever folder it is given, or
        the user's home folder when that doesn't exist.

    navigation:
        The bar holds an up button, disabled at the top of the file system, and
        a home button, each with a tooltip, then the current folder's path as a
        breadcrumb, where clicking any folder along it goes straight there. At
        the right end, a "Show hidden" checkbox, off by default, shows the
        entries whose names start with a dot. Its label is shown in full, never
        cut off below its baseline.

    entries:
        The current folder's entries, each a row with an icon and its name: a
        folder icon for a folder, and a file icon for a file. Folders come
        first, then files, each sorted by name ignoring case, with a
        @{ScrollbarScope}. Clicking a row selects it, highlighted; clicking it
        twice opens it. Clicking a folder twice goes into it. A folder that
        can't be read says why in place of the list. Going to another folder
        clears the selection and scrolls back to the top. Which entries are
        listed is up to each kind of browser.

    newFolder:
        The bar holds a "New Folder" button with a folder-plus icon, after the
        home button, whose tooltip says it creates a folder in the current one.
        Pressing it opens a row at the top of the list, above the entries: a
        folder icon, a name field with focus, a "Create" button, and a cancel
        button. <Enter> in the field creates the folder, as does Create; the
        cancel button, or <Escape> in the field, closes the row without creating
        anything, and <Escape> there doesn't close whatever the browser sits in.
        Going to another folder closes the row too.

        The name can't be empty, contain a slash or backslash, be "." or "..",
        or be the name of something already in the current folder; while it is
        one of those, once something has been typed, the row says why beneath
        the field and Create is disabled. A folder that can't be created says
        why the same way, and the row stays open.

        Once created, the row closes and the new folder is listed in its place
        among the others, selected and scrolled into view.

    keyboard:
        overview:
            The browser can be used entirely from the keyboard, the way a
            platform's own file dialog can. It takes focus when it opens, on its
            list, and the selected entry is always scrolled into view. <Tab> and
            <Shift+Tab> move between its controls: the bar's buttons, the
            breadcrumb, the checkbox, the list, and the footer's buttons. The
            keys below work wherever focus is in the browser, except while
            typing a new folder's name, where the name field has the keys.

        movingTheSelection:
            <Down> selects the next entry and <Up> the previous one; with
            nothing selected, <Down> selects the first and <Up> the last.
            Neither goes past the ends. <Home> selects the first entry and
            <End> the last. <Page Down> and <Page Up> move ten entries at a
            time, stopping at the ends.

        typeToSelect:
            Typing letters, digits, or other characters selects the first entry
            whose name starts with what has been typed, ignoring case. What is
            typed builds up while the keys come less than a second apart, and
            starts afresh after a longer pause. Typing that matches no entry
            leaves the selection where it is.

        going:
            <Enter> or <Right> opens the selected entry, as clicking it twice
            does. <Backspace>, <Left>, or <Alt+Up> (<Cmd+Up> on macOS) goes up
            to the folder holding the current one, and selects the folder just
            left, so going in and back out returns to where it was. <Alt+Home>
            (<Cmd+Shift+H> on macOS) goes to the home folder.

        choosingAndCancelling:
            <Ctrl+Enter> (<Cmd+Enter> on macOS) chooses, as the Choose button
            does. <Escape> leaves without choosing, as Cancel does, once there
            is no new folder row open to close first.

        otherCommands:
            <Ctrl+H> (<Cmd+Shift+.> on macOS) switches Show hidden, keeping the
            selected entry selected when it is still listed. <Ctrl+Shift+N>
            (<Cmd+Shift+N> on macOS) opens the new folder row. Each button's
            tooltip gives its shortcut.

    choosing:
        The footer shows what will be chosen, then "Cancel", which leaves
        without choosing, and a primary "Choose", which chooses it, disabled
        while there is nothing to choose. What can be chosen is up to each kind
        of browser.
